#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-venue — the PRODUCER for `cc-backlog claim --venue local|cloud`.

bin/cc-dispatch:470 states the gap in its own words:

    `cc-backlog claim --venue local|cloud` shipped fully built and fully tested with
    ZERO PRODUCERS.

Everything downstream of the decision exists and is tested — `fire_venue` reads the venue off the
argv that will actually run, `cloud_declare` makes the off-box session observable, the claim gate
refuses ineligible work, the fold keys the liveness oracles off the label. Measured 2026-08-11:
**0 of 294 open items carried a cloud venue.** Nothing decided, so nothing went.

This is the thing that decides. It writes `venuePlan` + `venueWhy` onto open rows
(`cc-backlog venue`), and cc-dispatch reads the plan at the one seam where a fire is composed.

── WHAT IT IS NOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NOT A KEYWORD ROUTER, and the plan says why with numbers rather than principle
(CLOUD_BACKLOG_PIPELINE.md §2). Of four briefs triaged by hand, one was rejected outright — it
patches the fleet spawn rail and its premise had gone stale against the same day's landings — and
one needed 215 commits of history inlined before a VM could attempt it. **A producer that routed
on keywords would have sent both.** So the decision is four separate questions, and only the first
is a word list:

    1. does the specification NAME state a VM cannot see?      → bin/cc-eligible spelling classes
    2. does it CITE history the shallow clone cannot reach?    → bin/cc-eligible measured arm
    3. is its premise still standing against TODAY's tree?     → bin/cc-premise, re-run here
    4. can this box CERTIFY 2 at all?                          → the horizon oracle state

NOT A SECOND ELIGIBILITY IMPLEMENTATION. Questions 1, 2 and 4 are answered by importing
`bin/cc-eligible` — the module object, not a re-derivation of its rules — because that same file is
what REFUSES at `cc-backlog claim --venue cloud`. A report and a refusal that can disagree is the
defect the whole predicate was factored out to prevent, and a producer is the easiest place in the
system to re-create it by accident.

── THE ASYMMETRY: THE GATE FAILS OPEN, THE PRODUCER FAILS CLOSED ────────────────────────────────
These look contradictory and are not. cc-eligible fails OPEN on anything it cannot read, because a
classifier that refused on "I could not tell" would block the whole cloud tap on a missing file,
and because a wrong INELIGIBLE only leaves an item exactly where it already is — claimable locally,
losing nothing.

The producer is the other side of that trade. It is the thing that PROMOTES work into a venue where
a wrong answer cannot be seen: a wrongly-routed item burns an account's quota, improvises a
plausible answer against history it cannot read, and reports success (§5, the anti-goal). So it
requires a POSITIVE certification for every cloud label, and routes local — with the reason
recorded — on every uncertainty. Refusing to promote costs nothing; promoting blind costs quota and
produces a confident wrong answer.

🚨 THE GUARD, KEYED ON THE DANGEROUS EFFECT. A cloud VM must never build or run the venue rule: it
would be deciding its own admission, and its 50-commit clone cannot read the history that justifies
the exclusions. The enforcement is NOT "refuse to run somewhere unusual" — a guard keyed on being
in a strange place fires on its own test harness and misses the real case (memory:
guard-refusal-fires-on-its-own-harness). It is keyed on the effect: **a cloud label may only be
written from a certification, and a shallow clone cannot certify.** `HistoryOracle.certify()`
returns `shallow` on a grafted repo — where every resolvable sha is inside its own horizon by
construction, so the reach arm would answer REACHABLE to everything — and `--apply` will not write
`cloud` without `ok`. On a VM that is automatic and needs no rule to be remembered.

Verbs:
  assess <id> [--json]    the decision for ONE item. Never writes. exit 0 always.
  run [--apply] [--json] [--project P] [--limit N]
                          decide over every OPEN item. DRY BY DEFAULT — prints the plan and the
                          counts; `--apply` writes the labels via `cc-backlog venue`.

Env:
  CC_VENUE_BACKLOG_BIN / CC_VENUE_ELIGIBLE_BIN / CC_VENUE_PREMISE_BIN — sibling resolution overrides
  CC_VENUE_PREMISE=off   — skip question 3 (for a fixtured test with no repo to ask about)
  CC_ELIGIBLE_REPO, CC_ELIGIBLE_HISTORY_DEPTH — read by the imported predicate, not by this file
"""

import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))


def _resolve(env, name):
    return os.environ.get(env) or os.path.join(HERE, name)


def _load_eligible():
    """Import bin/cc-eligible AS A MODULE — the same code object the claim gate runs.

    By path, because it is an extension-less executable: `import cc_eligible` cannot find it and a
    copy of its rules here would be the exact drift this file exists to avoid.
    """
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    path = _resolve("CC_VENUE_ELIGIBLE_BIN", "cc-eligible")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        sys.stderr.write("cc-venue: cannot find the shared predicate at %s — refusing to route "
                         "from a second implementation\n" % path)
        sys.exit(3)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(
        "cc_eligible", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("cc_eligible", path)
    )
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


CE = _load_eligible()

# The closed set of `venueWhy` tokens. Every why is `"<token>: <detail>"`, so `split(": ")[0]` is
# the class and the rest is the evidence — the same head-biased shape every verdict in this stack
# uses, for the same reason (a machine reads the first field, a human reads the line).
WHY_ELIGIBLE = "eligible"
WHY_UNCERTIFIED = "uncertified-history"
PREMISE_PREFIX = "premise-"

# cc-premise verdicts that are NOT "the premise is standing". `clear` is the only one this file
# treats as a green light, which is STRICTER than cc-premise's own gate (it blocks on `superseded`,
# `self-duplicate` and `falsified` and advises on the rest). Deliberately stricter, and only here:
# cc-premise advises because its consumer is a worker whose BRIEF carries the correction, so an
# item with a decayed sub-claim still gets done correctly. A cloud worker has no such reader — the
# correction lives in this box's ledger and the VM cannot see the ledger at all. `suspect` in
# particular means "a cited sha or path no longer resolves", which is the same class of unreadable
# reference that the shallow clone turns into a confident wrong answer.
PREMISE_OK = "clear"


def _run(argv, timeout=60, stdin=None):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=timeout,
            input=stdin.encode() if stdin else None,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None, "", ""
    return p.returncode, p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace"), p.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace")


def premise_verdict(item_id):
    """cc-premise's own verdict token for this item, or None when it could not be asked.

    None and a verdict are different answers and the caller must not collapse them: an unasked
    premise is an uncertainty (route local, say so), while `clear` is the positive certification
    this file requires before promoting anything.
    """
    if os.environ.get("CC_VENUE_PREMISE") == "off":
        return "skipped"
    rc, out, _err = _run([_resolve("CC_VENUE_PREMISE_BIN", "cc-premise"), "check", item_id])
    if rc is None:
        return None
    line = (out.splitlines() or [""])[0].strip()
    if not line.startswith("verdict="):
        return None
    return line[len("verdict="):].strip() or None


def _clip(s, n):
    return s if len(s) <= n else s[: n - 1] + "…"


def build_index():
    """The shared index — with cc-eligible's FAIL-OPEN converted to this file's FAIL-CLOSED.

    `load_records` reaches `_open_unknown` on an unreadable ledger, which prints `verdict=unknown-…`
    and exits 0. That is exactly right inside a claim (never starve the queue on a missing file) and
    exactly wrong here: exit 0 from a producer reads as "considered everything, routed nothing",
    which is indistinguishable from a clean store with no cloud work in it. Same predicate, opposite
    failure direction, made explicit at the boundary rather than by re-implementing the reader.
    """
    try:
        return CE.build_index(CE.load_records())
    except SystemExit:
        sys.stderr.write("cc-venue: the backlog ledger could not be read — refusing to route "
                         "(cc-eligible fails OPEN here on purpose; a producer must not)\n")
        sys.exit(3)


def decide(item_id, idx):
    """{venue, why, token, detail, eligible, premise} — the whole routing decision for one item.

    ORDERED CHEAPEST-FIRST and short-circuiting, which is not only a performance choice: an item
    already refused for naming `launchd` must not have its premise re-run, because running a
    falsifier is a side effect (cc-premise executes stored probes) and spending one on work that
    can never go off-box is a cost with no possible verdict attached.
    """
    full = CE.assess_full(item_id, idx)
    hist = full["history"]

    if full["verdict"] == CE.UNKNOWN_ITEM:
        return {"id": item_id, "venue": "local", "token": CE.UNKNOWN_ITEM,
                "detail": "no such item in the ledger", "eligible": full, "premise": None}

    if full["classes"]:
        # EVERY class that fired, not just the winner. The winner is what the gate will say; the
        # rest is what a reader needs to know before arguing with it, and it costs one line.
        # The FIRST class is already the token, so its name is not repeated in the detail — a why
        # reading `ineligible-offbox-lane: ineligible-offbox-lane (off-box)` spends its most
        # readable field saying nothing. Siblings keep their names, because there the name is the
        # only thing distinguishing them.
        parts = [",".join(full["classes"][0]["tokens"][:4])]
        parts += ["also %s (%s)" % (c["verdict"], ",".join(c["tokens"][:4]))
                  for c in full["classes"][1:]]
        return {"id": item_id, "venue": "local", "token": full["verdict"],
                "detail": _clip("; ".join(parts), 300), "eligible": full, "premise": None}

    if hist["state"] != "ok":
        # THE GUARD. Nothing named local-only state and nothing cited unreachable history — but the
        # second of those is only meaningful if the horizon could be measured, and here it could
        # not. Route local and say which way the instrument failed.
        return {"id": item_id, "venue": "local", "token": WHY_UNCERTIFIED,
                "detail": _clip("%s — %s (repo=%s)" % (
                    hist["state"], CE.HISTORY_NOTE.get(hist["state"], ""), hist["repo"] or "none"),
                    300),
                "eligible": full, "premise": None}

    pv = premise_verdict(item_id)
    if pv != PREMISE_OK and pv != "skipped":
        return {"id": item_id, "venue": "local", "token": PREMISE_PREFIX + (pv or "unreadable"),
                "detail": _clip(
                    "cc-premise says %s — the premise has moved against the current tree, and a "
                    "cloud worker cannot read the correction (it lives in this ledger)"
                    % (pv or "nothing readable"), 300),
                "eligible": full, "premise": pv}

    return {
        "id": item_id, "venue": "cloud", "token": WHY_ELIGIBLE,
        "detail": _clip(
            "no local-only spelling fired; every cited sha is inside the newest %d commits of %s "
            "(certified from a full clone at %s); premise %s"
            % (hist["depth"], hist["ref"], hist["repo"], pv), 300),
        "eligible": full, "premise": pv,
    }


def why_of(d):
    return "%s: %s" % (d["token"], d["detail"])


def _open_items():
    exe = _resolve("CC_VENUE_BACKLOG_BIN", "cc-backlog")
    rc, out, _err = _run([exe, "list", "--all", "--json"], timeout=180)
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    try:
        return json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def write_label(item_id, venue, why):
    """`cc-backlog venue` is the ONE writer. Returns (ok, message).

    Shelling out rather than appending a line here, so the closed-set check, the done-item refusal
    and the idempotence live in exactly one place — the actuator is the arbiter (memory:
    make-the-actuator-the-arbiter).
    """
    exe = _resolve("CC_VENUE_BACKLOG_BIN", "cc-backlog")
    rc, _out, err = _run([exe, "venue", item_id, "--venue", venue, "--why", why])
    return rc == 0, (err.strip().splitlines() or [""])[-1]


def cmd_assess(item_id, as_json):
    idx = build_index()
    d = decide(item_id, idx)
    if as_json:
        out = {k: d[k] for k in ("id", "venue", "token", "detail")}
        out["why"] = why_of(d)
        out["history"] = d["eligible"]["history"]
        out["classes"] = d["eligible"]["classes"]
        out["premise"] = d["premise"]
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(out, indent=2) + "\n")
        sys.exit(0)
    w = sys.stdout.write
    w("%s  %s\n" % (d["id"], _clip(d["eligible"]["title"], 110)))
    w("  venue : %s\n" % d["venue"])
    w("  why   : %s\n" % why_of(d))
    sys.exit(0)


def cmd_run(argv):
    apply_ = "--apply" in argv
    as_json = "--json" in argv
    project = limit = None
    for i, a in enumerate(argv):
        if a == "--project" and i + 1 < len(argv):
            project = argv[i + 1]
        if a == "--limit" and i + 1 < len(argv):
            try:
                limit = int(argv[i + 1])
            except ValueError:
                sys.stderr.write("cc-venue run: --limit wants an integer\n")
                sys.exit(2)

    items = _open_items()
    if items is None:
        sys.stderr.write("cc-venue: `cc-backlog list` failed — refusing to route blind\n")
        sys.exit(3)
    idx = build_index()

    # OPEN ONLY. `claimed` work already has a holder and a venue that the fold keys the liveness
    # oracles off; `blocked` work is outside every dispatch wave by construction, so routing it
    # would be a decision no dispatcher will ever read.
    rows = [i for i in items if i.get("status") == "open"]
    if project:
        rows = [i for i in rows if i.get("project") == project]
    if limit is not None:
        rows = rows[:limit]

    # PRE-WARM the oracle per project, exactly as the census does: one `cat-file --batch-check` for
    # the whole store instead of one per item.
    from collections import defaultdict
    by_project = defaultdict(list)
    for it in rows:
        ent = idx.get(it["id"])
        if ent:
            by_project[ent.get("project", "")].append(ent["text"])
    for proj, texts in by_project.items():
        orc = CE.oracle_for(proj)
        if orc.certify() == "ok":
            toks = set()
            for t in texts:
                toks.update(CE.HEX.findall(t))
            orc.resolve_many(toks)

    decisions, counts = [], defaultdict(int)
    for it in rows:
        d = decide(it["id"], idx)
        d["title"] = (it.get("title") or "")[:120]
        d["project"] = it.get("project") or ""
        d["written"] = None
        counts[d["venue"]] += 1
        counts["token:" + d["token"]] += 1
        if apply_:
            # A COST GATE, and STRICTLY WEAKER than the idempotence it shadows (memory:
            # cost-gate-must-be-strictly-weaker). `cc-backlog venue` already declines to append an
            # unchanged decision — it is the arbiter and stays so — but reaching that decline costs
            # a full fold of the ledger PER ITEM, and the ledger is append-only. Measured on the
            # live store: 302 items over 8,100 records is ~20 minutes, essentially all of it spent
            # re-deriving state this loop already holds in `items`.
            #
            # So the skip fires ONLY on an exact match of both fields against the same fold the
            # actuator would read, which is a subset of the cases the actuator itself would skip.
            # It can never admit a write the actuator would refuse, and it can never suppress one
            # the actuator would take: the sole effect is not paying to be told "unchanged".
            if (it.get("venuePlan") or "", it.get("venueWhy") or "") == (d["venue"], why_of(d)):
                d["written"] = "unchanged"
                counts["unchanged"] += 1
            else:
                ok, msg = write_label(it["id"], d["venue"], why_of(d))
                d["written"] = ok
                if not ok:
                    counts["write-failed"] += 1
                    sys.stderr.write("cc-venue: %s NOT labelled — %s\n" % (it["id"], msg))
        decisions.append(d)

    horizons = {}
    for proj in by_project:
        orc = CE.oracle_for(proj)
        if orc.repo:
            horizons[orc.repo] = {"state": orc.state, "ref": orc.ref, "depth": orc.depth}

    if as_json:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({
            "applied": apply_, "considered": len(rows),
            "counts": dict(counts), "horizons": horizons,
            "decisions": [{k: d[k] for k in
                           ("id", "project", "title", "venue", "token", "detail", "written")}
                          for d in decisions],
        }, indent=2) + "\n")
        sys.exit(0)

    w = sys.stdout.write
    w("cc-venue run — %d open item(s)%s%s\n"
      % (len(rows), " · project=%s" % project if project else "",
         " · APPLIED" if apply_ else " · DRY RUN (nothing written; add --apply)"))
    for repo, h in sorted(horizons.items()):
        w("  horizon: %-52s %s (ref=%s depth=%s)\n"
          % (repo, h["state"], h["ref"] or "-", h["depth"]))
    w("\n  venue=cloud : %d\n  venue=local : %d\n" % (counts["cloud"], counts["local"]))
    if counts.get("write-failed"):
        w("  WRITE FAILED: %d — see stderr\n" % counts["write-failed"])
    w("\n  why tokens:\n")
    for k in sorted(k for k in counts if k.startswith("token:")):
        w("    %-30s %d\n" % (k[len("token:"):], counts[k]))
    # THE CLOUD BUCKET IS PRINTED IN FULL, and it is the inverse of the census this file leans on:
    # cc-eligible prints its ELIGIBLE bucket because a missed refusal is the silent error THERE. Here
    # the promotion is the act, so the promoted set is what a reader must be able to audit line by
    # line before quota is spent on it.
    cloud = [d for d in decisions if d["venue"] == "cloud"]
    w("\n── venue=cloud — READ THESE BEFORE THEY FIRE ─────────────────────────────\n")
    if not cloud:
        w("  (none)\n")
    for d in cloud:
        w("  %s [%s] %s\n" % (d["id"], d["project"], d["title"]))
        w("      %s\n" % why_of(d))
    sys.exit(0)


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        sys.stdout.write(__doc__)
        sys.exit(0)
    verb, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    if verb == "assess":
        if not rest:
            sys.stderr.write("cc-venue assess: <id> is required\n")
            sys.exit(2)
        cmd_assess(rest[0], "--json" in rest)
    elif verb == "run":
        cmd_run(rest)
    else:
        sys.stderr.write("cc-venue: unknown verb %s\n" % verb)
        sys.exit(2)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
